mod:call_result_slice_carrier_chain;
// DEF-317's POINTER CHAINS (1.6.1e, landing 166; management_4's condition cells of 2026-10-29 03:06, re-cut
// 04:18 through pointer FIELDS): a call-result slice handed out of a struct carrier reached through a CHAIN
// of pointers, and the carrier reader's two edges. The carrier reader (`escape_carrier_runs`) follows a ref
// that is a carrier -- a struct that may hold a run, a pointer, a run reached THROUGH a pointer -- into its
// chain one pointer LINK at a time (a pointer field `c2.c1p`, a pointer local), four units of fuel, and
// FAILS CLOSED: a chain still to follow when the fuel is out is UNRESOLVED, refused as BORROW-009 at the
// slice where the escape pass records the write (`escape_unresolved_slice`; the aliasing pass's
// `check_slice_targets` adds no second line for a node the escape pass reported), never read as "no
// targets". The chain here is `C1 = { BS->:bp }`, `C2 = { C1->:c1p }`, `C3 = { C2->:c2p }`, `C4 = { C3->:c3p }`,
// read by auto-dereferencing getters (`getslc3(C3->:q) = q.c2p.c1p.bp.sl`): three links resolve (site 1,
// c3 -> c2 -> c1 -> b); four do not (sites 2, 3) -- site 3 is the accepted over-refusal past the fuel, a
// safe shape (no view live) refused in the closed direction (cell d4n, 0 tree sites by the sweep). A
// getter that reads the chain through an EXPLICIT dereference (`pass (<-(<-q)).sl`) records no ref at all
// and is SILENT at every depth: DEF-324 (OPEN, HIGH, landing 167), recorded by probe id (cells d1x, d3x,
// d4x), not a site here. The own-holder readers (`escape_slice_own_holder_rootless` (ii), and the named
// road's DEF-305 (b)) resolve ONE pointer link, so a chain of two or more links with NO view live finds no
// own holder and the write meets the carrier's own view: site 14 (three links, no view) and site 16 (two
// links BY NAME, `wsl(c2.c1p.bp.sl)`, no view) are DEF-325's over-refusals (LOW; parity: the call-result
// cell and its by-name twin refuse alike on 166), where site 15 -- the two-link chain by name under a view
// -- was SILENT on landing 165 (113 at both legs: DEF-305 (b)'s through-pointer reader resolved one link
// and the refs loop's target sat at the pointer), a by-name face of DEF-317 that the carrier reader closes
// (cells d2b, d3b). A pointer root with TWO pointees -- assigned two carriers in branches (sites 4, 5),
// re-pointed before the call (sites 8, 9) -- resolves to no single own holder, so the write meets the
// carrier's own view even with no other view live: the NAMED road's verdict today (`wsl(bp.sl)` with two
// carriers is BORROW-015 with or without a view), the two-pointee class of D-286's list; site 8 is safe at
// run time (the write lands in `p2.arr`, 0/0) and refused by the flow-insensitive chain as its by-name
// twin is (the re-pointed class, c306f/k302a). A COPY of the carrier's slice written (sites 6, 7, 10-13)
// meets the carrier's own view: parity with `H2[]:s2 = b.sl; s2[0i64].o = …`, refused by name with or
// without a view (the slice-copy class). Sites 10-13 are DEF-317's BY-NAME face: the pointer's slice field
// copied by name (`H2[]:s2 = bp.sl;`) was silent (113 at both legs under a view) because DEF-305 (b)'s
// through-pointer reader looked inward from the slice BASE only (`s2` has no pointer on its path) and the
// refs loop's target was rooted at the POINTER, where no view is rooted; the carrier reader follows the
// ref through the pointer. Every site here compiled on landing 165's compiler
// (`wt/51a/.internal/probes/t166/cells3/`, `res165/cells3_165.txt`); the hazard sites read freed memory
// at both legs (113). The clean one-link twins -- the carrier through a named pointer local, behind a
// pointer field -- are controls 19 and 20 of `tests/backend/programs/call_result_deref_receiver_slice_ok.npk`.
error:E1;
